WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.060
Can an attacker reach us
through a vendor we trust?

00:00:03.435 --> 00:00:04.845
Yes, in four ways.

00:00:06.217 --> 00:00:09.407
First, the update we trusted
carried the attacker in.

00:00:09.587 --> 00:00:13.497
The poisoned version is signed
with the vendor's real certificate.

00:00:13.937 --> 00:00:16.287
Our own systems install it on schedule.

00:00:16.687 --> 00:00:20.297
One compromised vendor reaches
every customer at once.

00:00:20.622 --> 00:00:24.592
Tools with the deepest access
are the most likely to be abused.

00:00:25.717 --> 00:00:28.387
Second, our supplier's key opens our door.

00:00:28.657 --> 00:00:32.227
Our IT partner holds a standing
login into our systems.

00:00:32.622 --> 00:00:36.152
Often it is one shared account,
valid around the clock,

00:00:36.322 --> 00:00:38.192
with more rights than the job needs.

00:00:38.607 --> 00:00:39.877
Break into one supplier,

00:00:40.287 --> 00:00:43.197
and the intruder holds the
keys of dozens of companies.

00:00:43.597 --> 00:00:46.007
Every login looks like
routine maintenance.

00:00:47.217 --> 00:00:50.047
Third, our software is
built from strangers' code.

00:00:50.347 --> 00:00:54.477
Our app is a thin layer on top of
hundreds of open-source components.

00:00:54.957 --> 00:00:58.587
When a maintainer is tricked,
or hands the project to a stranger,

00:00:59.097 --> 00:01:01.507
the next version can carry malicious code.

00:01:01.837 --> 00:01:03.187
It runs under our name.

00:01:03.502 --> 00:01:06.112
And without a list of components,
we cannot answer:

00:01:06.502 --> 00:01:07.292
are we affected?

00:01:08.417 --> 00:01:10.927
Fourth, their breach,
but the data is ours.

00:01:11.372 --> 00:01:14.642
Payroll records and the
customer list live with vendors.

00:01:15.037 --> 00:01:17.027
Those vendors run on other vendors.

00:01:17.462 --> 00:01:21.552
When one is breached, the data leaks
without a single packet touching us.

00:01:21.747 --> 00:01:26.057
Under GDPR, the breach
notification clock is still ours.

00:01:27.183 --> 00:01:28.393
What good looks like.

00:01:28.678 --> 00:01:33.128
One list of every supplier that holds
our data or can reach our systems.

00:01:33.533 --> 00:01:37.263
Contract clauses cover breach
notification and audit rights.

00:01:37.573 --> 00:01:40.643
Supplier logins are named
accounts that expire.

00:01:41.018 --> 00:01:43.228
Updates reach a small test group first.

00:01:43.613 --> 00:01:48.043
A software bill of materials alerts
us the day a component turns bad.

00:01:48.318 --> 00:01:52.088
This is mostly a list,
a contract and a habit, not a product.

00:01:52.388 --> 00:01:56.638
NIS2 names supply chain security
as a risk management measure.

00:01:58.363 --> 00:01:59.573
A suggested first step.

00:01:59.858 --> 00:02:03.268
Ask which suppliers can reach what,
and who owns that list.

00:02:03.598 --> 00:02:07.208
If nobody can produce it within the hour,
that is the finding.

00:02:08.817 --> 00:02:10.587
Four ways trust gets abused.

00:02:10.997 --> 00:02:13.987
Know the list,
and write the clauses before you sign.

